Perfect! I found the answer in the log file. According to the post-mortem discussion:

**Over-engineering decision that created compliance violations:**
- Storing full request payloads in logs created PII (Personally Identifiable Information) violations

**Basic approach that should have been used instead:**
- Simple action metadata logging was sufficient

The issue was that by over-engineering the logging system to capture complete request payloads, they inadvertently stored sensitive personal information, violating compliance requirements. A simpler approach of just logging action metadata would have avoided this problem entirely.
